---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';

// Parked 2026-09-14 (research/site-audit-2026-09-14.md) and rewritten the same day so that
// restoring it is a rename. Before adding or rewording an open question below, check it
// against the unresolved ledger (is it still open?) and the interview guide (does the wording
// hand Larry any detail a question to him is meant to draw out?). Larry reads this page.
// No evidence badges here: scripts/check-published.mjs fails on them, so the scale is words.
---

<BaseLayout title="Research">
  <span class="eyebrow">Method and open questions</span>
  <h1>Research</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This site is a memoir on top and an archive underneath. Larry's memories are primary-source
    evidence — recorded exactly as given, never rewritten. Military and historical records are
    gathered alongside them. When the two disagree, both are kept, side by side.
  </p>

  <h2>The evidence scale</h2>
  <p>Every important claim in the archive's records carries one of five classifications:</p>
  <dl class="scale">
    <div>
      <dt>Confirmed</dt>
      <dd>Established by contemporary documents or unambiguous records.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Strongly supported</dt>
      <dd>Multiple independent lines of evidence point the same way.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Plausible</dt>
      <dd>Consistent with known facts; a working hypothesis, not a finding.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Unverified recollection</dt>
      <dd>Larry's memory, preserved as given, not yet matched to records.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Contradicted</dt>
      <dd>Research disagrees with the memory. The memory is still preserved.</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <h2>Asking Larry</h2>
  <p>
    The best research tool this project has is Larry himself. What he tells the family, at the
    table, on the telephone, or in the comment box under a photograph, goes into the archive in
    his own words, with the date he said it. Anything he remembers is welcome, whenever it comes
    back to him; <a href={withBase('/memories/')}>Tell a Story</a> is the place for anything that
    does not belong to one picture.
  </p>

  <h2>The open questions</h2>
  <p>
    Six questions that would open the most doors. Many narrower ones are tracked alongside them,
    and several wait on the service records the family has requested. Last reviewed
    <time datetime="2026-09-14">14 September 2026</time>.
  </p>
  <ol class="questions">
    <li>
      <strong>The killed-in-action report.</strong> In the autumn of 1965, in the Army hospital at
      Camp Zama in Japan, Larry learned that <em>Stars and Stripes</em> had listed him as killed
      in action, and saw his name in the paper's list of the dead. Find the item. No Hunnewell,
      in any spelling, is among the American dead of the whole war (the national casualty file,
      checked name by name in September 2026), so a casualty listing under his name is itself
      the error. What has been searched and found nothing: the Pacific <em>Stars and
      Stripes</em> for 1964&ndash;66, under his name and eleven ways a scan can garble it; every
      Vietnam casualty list that paper printed from September to December 1965, read one by
      one; and the American newspapers digitised on newspapers.com for 1962&ndash;66. Not yet
      searched: the Inglewood hometown paper, which has not been found in digitised form.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>His unit.</strong> Larry's discharge paper names only his last assignment, in
      Japan, not the outfit he served with in Vietnam. Identify it. His service file is the
      record most likely to name it: the request to the National Personnel Records Center was
      mailed on 17 August 2026, its delivery was confirmed in September, and no reply has come
      yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>&ldquo;Ton Son Nuht.&rdquo;</strong> Larry spelled it that way himself. Identify the
      headquarters base he remembers; T&acirc;n S&#417;n Nh&#7913;t, outside Saigon, is the
      leading hypothesis and is not yet confirmed. His spelling is phonetic, as you would expect
      of a name he only ever heard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sgt. Lowry / Lowery.</strong> Identify the Special Forces sergeant Larry served
      beside on N&uacute;i B&agrave; &#272;en, who ran the camp when Captain Kennedy was away. No
      Lowry, Lowery or Lowrey among the American war dead could be him (the national casualty
      file, checked September 2026), so he most likely came home. The search has moved from
      memorials to Special Forces team rosters and veterans&rsquo; associations.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Captain Geutler.</strong> That is Larry&rsquo;s own spelling. The officer he called
      when he needed the helicopter Rosethorn 007, and the pilot who flew him off the mountain.
      Larry has picked him out in a photograph, <a href={withBase('/photos/vn-0020/')}>VN-0020</a>.
      Identify him. Searches so far, made under spellings the archive had before Larry spelled
      the name himself, have not found him.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bombs in the ocean.</strong> Among the first things the family wrote down from
      Larry&rsquo;s memories: stories of aircraft dumping bombs into the sea rather than dropping
      them on their targets. Establish what lay behind them &mdash; routine procedure for aircraft
      that could not land loaded, a rule for aborted missions, a consequence of limits on targets,
      or rumour of the time. Naval air operations manuals and Seventh Fleet records would settle
      it. No research has been done on this one yet.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Source priorities</h2>
  <p>
    Primary and contemporary sources first: Army and National Archives records, morning reports,
    operational and after-action reports, personnel records, contemporary <em>Stars and
    Stripes</em> issues, period maps and photographs, official casualty databases, and oral
    histories. Secondary histories inform context but do not override stronger contemporary
    evidence. Citations accumulate in <a href={withBase('/sources/')}>Sources</a>. A companion
    page catalogs the <a href={withBase('/research/resources/')}>public online resources</a> —
    free and paid — used so far, and what each one turned up.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }

  .scale {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .scale > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 15rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: start;
    padding: 0.6rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .scale dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }

  .scale dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .questions li {
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .scale > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.3rem;
    }
  }
</style>
